{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01C8hspmNKCQnpfgqjKpiYTP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QVHYMQURvYjtfTdeopR3PP","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind peer review of a paper submitted to EMNLP 2026 Findings. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Scoring Rubric (ACL Rolling Review, Feb 2025+)\n\n**Soundness (1-5):**\n- 5 = Excellent: one of the most thorough studies I've seen\n- 4 = Strong: sufficient support for all claims; some extra experiments nice but not essential\n- 3 = Acceptable: sufficient support for main claims\n- 2 = Weak: some claims not well-supported\n- 1 = Poor: claims not supported\n\n**Excitement (1-5):**\n- 5 = Highly Exciting\n- 4 = Exciting\n- 3 = Moderately Exciting\n- 2 = Mildly Exciting\n- 1 = Not Exciting\n\n**Overall Assessment (1-5):**\n- 5 = Largely complete, no clear revision points\n- 4 = Minor points to revise\n- 3 = Major points to revise\n- 2 = Significant revisions needed\n- 1 = Not publishable even after revisions\n\nEMNLP Findings acceptance rate is approximately 20-25%. Findings papers are expected to present solid empirical contributions even if not of the novelty level required for the main conference.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Calibration Considered Harmful? Evidence That Behavioral Correction Fails Across Scales in LLM User Simulation\"\n\n**Core claim:** Prompt-based (BCP) and training-based (DPO) calibration methods fail to correct dialect-dependent behavioral biases in LLM user simulators on τ-bench retail domain.\n\n**Models:** Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct\n\n**Three findings:**\n1. **FM1 (Completion collapse at 7B):** BCP reduces task completion by 65-73% at 7B. Replicates 4/4 runs. Bootstrap 95% CI: [-82, -55]%, p < 0.001. Unambiguous.\n2. **C3 (Scale-dependent severity):** 7B: catastrophic failure. 14B: immune to side effects but uncalibrated. Scale completion difference CI: [1, 49] pp, excludes zero.\n3. **FM4 (DPO-BCP interaction reversal, suggestive):** Calibration direction reverses in 10/12 variant×run checks when DPO+BCP combined. Sign test p=0.039, but magnitude CI [-3, +37] pp crosses zero. Reported as suggestive.\n\n**Additional observations (demoted):** FM2 (cross-dimension interference) and FM3 (overcorrection rebound) are acknowledged as unreliable/confounded and not presented as confirmed mechanisms.\n\n**Methods:**\n- τ-bench retail domain, 20 multi-turn tool-calling tasks, 16 tools\n- 3 behavioral dimensions: completion rate, politeness score, question rate\n- 6 BCP variants per dialect: baseline, AAVE, Indian English, overcorrected, single-dimension (2)\n- DPO training on Qwen2.5-7B with dialect-calibrated preference pairs (LoRA, DPO-003)\n- 4 independent runs × 20 tasks = 80 episodes per condition\n- BCa bootstrap with 10,000 resamples, stratified by run\n- Thresholds: 80% baseline retention (completion), 30% gap reduction (calibration)\n\n**Key tables:**\n- Table 1: 7B Base BCP completion/politeness across 4 runs\n- Table 2: 7B vs 14B comparison\n- Table 3: DPO-BCP interaction\n- Table 4: Bootstrap 95% CI summary (7 comparisons)\n- Table 5: Mechanism survival scorecard\n\n**Limitations acknowledged:** Single model family (Qwen2.5), single task domain (τ-bench retail), 20 tasks per run (wide CIs), missing 14B DPO experiment, underpowered secondary findings.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference\n\nThe paper was designed with the following experiments:\n- **plan_005b (7B BCP Multi-Run Replication):** 3 additional runs of 7B base BCP. Result: FM1 confirmed (aave retention 35.1%, indian 44.5%), FM2 exposed as high-variance artifact (SD=13.1%), FM3 collapsed (mean +0.4pp, 2/4 direction). Multi-run data undermined FM2/FM3 as named mechanisms.\n- **plan_005 (Bootstrap CI):** BCa bootstrap on existing data. FM1 CI excludes zero. FM3 CI [-2, 16.5]pp crosses zero. FM4 CI [-3, 37]pp crosses zero. Scale completion CI [1, 49]pp excludes zero but very wide. H2 gap reduction CI [12, 28]% — even upper bound below 30% threshold.\n- **plan_006 (14B DPO+BCP):** NOT EXECUTED due to GPU unavailability. FM4 reversal only documented at 7B scale. This is a significant gap — the most novel claim has the weakest evidence base.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed R1 review focused on:\n\n1. **Experimental design:** Are the experiments well-designed? Are the controls adequate? Is the evaluation methodology sound?\n2. **Statistical analysis:** Are the bootstrap CI methods appropriate? Are the thresholds (80% retention, 30% gap reduction) well-justified? Is the sample size (n=20 per run, 80 per condition) sufficient?\n3. **Results interpretation:** Do the authors overclaim or underclaim? Is the distinction between \"confirmed\" and \"suggestive\" findings appropriate?\n4. **Reproducibility:** Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are the DPO training details sufficient?\n5. **Missing experiments:** Impact of the missing 14B DPO experiment. Any other missing ablations?\n6. **Weaknesses in experimental coverage:** Single model family, single domain, etc.\n\nFormat your output as:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, numbered W1, W2, etc.]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Bulleted list]\n\n### Detailed Comments\n[Section-by-section analysis]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n\n### Confidence\n[1-5 scale: 5=absolutely certain, 4=quite sure, 3=fairly confident, 2=willing to be convinced, 1=guessing]\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":755,"cache_read_input_tokens":55766,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":755},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed833f14-0b34-43bd-911c-a079ebf54ca0","uuid":"e77f7fad-1740-4705-b5e3-67ba2762cc9d"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"acf9b1763d2407c04","tool_use_id":"toolu_01QVHYMQURvYjtfTdeopR3PP","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind peer review of a paper submitted to EMNLP 2026 Findings. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Scoring Rubric (ACL Rolling Review, Feb 2025+)\n\n**Soundness (1-5):**\n- 5 = Excellent: one of the most thorough studies I've seen\n- 4 = Strong: sufficient support for all claims; some extra experiments nice but not essential\n- 3 = Acceptable: sufficient support for main claims\n- 2 = Weak: some claims not well-supported\n- 1 = Poor: claims not supported\n\n**Excitement (1-5):**\n- 5 = Highly Exciting\n- 4 = Exciting\n- 3 = Moderately Exciting\n- 2 = Mildly Exciting\n- 1 = Not Exciting\n\n**Overall Assessment (1-5):**\n- 5 = Largely complete, no clear revision points\n- 4 = Minor points to revise\n- 3 = Major points to revise\n- 2 = Significant revisions needed\n- 1 = Not publishable even after revisions\n\nEMNLP Findings acceptance rate is approximately 20-25%. Findings papers are expected to present solid empirical contributions even if not of the novelty level required for the main conference.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Calibration Considered Harmful? Evidence That Behavioral Correction Fails Across Scales in LLM User Simulation\"\n\n**Core claim:** Prompt-based (BCP) and training-based (DPO) calibration methods fail to correct dialect-dependent behavioral biases in LLM user simulators on τ-bench retail domain.\n\n**Models:** Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct\n\n**Three findings:**\n1. **FM1 (Completion collapse at 7B):** BCP reduces task completion by 65-73% at 7B. Replicates 4/4 runs. Bootstrap 95% CI: [-82, -55]%, p < 0.001. Unambiguous.\n2. **C3 (Scale-dependent severity):** 7B: catastrophic failure. 14B: immune to side effects but uncalibrated. Scale completion difference CI: [1, 49] pp, excludes zero.\n3. **FM4 (DPO-BCP interaction reversal, suggestive):** Calibration direction reverses in 10/12 variant×run checks when DPO+BCP combined. Sign test p=0.039, but magnitude CI [-3, +37] pp crosses zero. Reported as suggestive.\n\n**Additional observations (demoted):** FM2 (cross-dimension interference) and FM3 (overcorrection rebound) are acknowledged as unreliable/confounded and not presented as confirmed mechanisms.\n\n**Methods:**\n- τ-bench retail domain, 20 multi-turn tool-calling tasks, 16 tools\n- 3 behavioral dimensions: completion rate, politeness score, question rate\n- 6 BCP variants per dialect: baseline, AAVE, Indian English, overcorrected, single-dimension (2)\n- DPO training on Qwen2.5-7B with dialect-calibrated preference pairs (LoRA, DPO-003)\n- 4 independent runs × 20 tasks = 80 episodes per condition\n- BCa bootstrap with 10,000 resamples, stratified by run\n- Thresholds: 80% baseline retention (completion), 30% gap reduction (calibration)\n\n**Key tables:**\n- Table 1: 7B Base BCP completion/politeness across 4 runs\n- Table 2: 7B vs 14B comparison\n- Table 3: DPO-BCP interaction\n- Table 4: Bootstrap 95% CI summary (7 comparisons)\n- Table 5: Mechanism survival scorecard\n\n**Limitations acknowledged:** Single model family (Qwen2.5), single task domain (τ-bench retail), 20 tasks per run (wide CIs), missing 14B DPO experiment, underpowered secondary findings.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference\n\nThe paper was designed with the following experiments:\n- **plan_005b (7B BCP Multi-Run Replication):** 3 additional runs of 7B base BCP. Result: FM1 confirmed (aave retention 35.1%, indian 44.5%), FM2 exposed as high-variance artifact (SD=13.1%), FM3 collapsed (mean +0.4pp, 2/4 direction). Multi-run data undermined FM2/FM3 as named mechanisms.\n- **plan_005 (Bootstrap CI):** BCa bootstrap on existing data. FM1 CI excludes zero. FM3 CI [-2, 16.5]pp crosses zero. FM4 CI [-3, 37]pp crosses zero. Scale completion CI [1, 49]pp excludes zero but very wide. H2 gap reduction CI [12, 28]% — even upper bound below 30% threshold.\n- **plan_006 (14B DPO+BCP):** NOT EXECUTED due to GPU unavailability. FM4 reversal only documented at 7B scale. This is a significant gap — the most novel claim has the weakest evidence base.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed R1 review focused on:\n\n1. **Experimental design:** Are the experiments well-designed? Are the controls adequate? Is the evaluation methodology sound?\n2. **Statistical analysis:** Are the bootstrap CI methods appropriate? Are the thresholds (80% retention, 30% gap reduction) well-justified? Is the sample size (n=20 per run, 80 per condition) sufficient?\n3. **Results interpretation:** Do the authors overclaim or underclaim? Is the distinction between \"confirmed\" and \"suggestive\" findings appropriate?\n4. **Reproducibility:** Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are the DPO training details sufficient?\n5. **Missing experiments:** Impact of the missing 14B DPO experiment. Any other missing ablations?\n6. **Weaknesses in experimental coverage:** Single model family, single domain, etc.\n\nFormat your output as:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, numbered W1, W2, etc.]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Bulleted list]\n\n### Detailed Comments\n[Section-by-section analysis]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n\n### Confidence\n[1-5 scale: 5=absolutely certain, 4=quite sure, 3=fairly confident, 2=willing to be convinced, 1=guessing]\n```\n","uuid":"c589fd4b-2bbf-4da8-8286-ec9b49eccff3","session_id":"ed833f14-0b34-43bd-911c-a079ebf54ca0"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind peer review of a paper submitted to EMNLP 2026 Findings. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Scoring Rubric (ACL Rolling Review, Feb 2025+)\n\n**Soundness (1-5):**\n- 5 = Excellent: one of the most thorough studies I've seen\n- 4 = Strong: sufficient support for all claims; some extra experiments nice but not essential\n- 3 = Acceptable: sufficient support for main claims\n- 2 = Weak: some claims not well-supported\n- 1 = Poor: claims not supported\n\n**Excitement (1-5):**\n- 5 = Highly Exciting\n- 4 = Exciting\n- 3 = Moderately Exciting\n- 2 = Mildly Exciting\n- 1 = Not Exciting\n\n**Overall Assessment (1-5):**\n- 5 = Largely complete, no clear revision points\n- 4 = Minor points to revise\n- 3 = Major points to revise\n- 2 = Significant revisions needed\n- 1 = Not publishable even after revisions\n\nEMNLP Findings acceptance rate is approximately 20-25%. Findings papers are expected to present solid empirical contributions even if not of the novelty level required for the main conference.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Calibration Considered Harmful? Evidence That Behavioral Correction Fails Across Scales in LLM User Simulation\"\n\n**Core claim:** Prompt-based (BCP) and training-based (DPO) calibration methods fail to correct dialect-dependent behavioral biases in LLM user simulators on τ-bench retail domain.\n\n**Models:** Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct\n\n**Three findings:**\n1. **FM1 (Completion collapse at 7B):** BCP reduces task completion by 65-73% at 7B. Replicates 4/4 runs. Bootstrap 95% CI: [-82, -55]%, p < 0.001. Unambiguous.\n2. **C3 (Scale-dependent severity):** 7B: catastrophic failure. 14B: immune to side effects but uncalibrated. Scale completion difference CI: [1, 49] pp, excludes zero.\n3. **FM4 (DPO-BCP interaction reversal, suggestive):** Calibration direction reverses in 10/12 variant×run checks when DPO+BCP combined. Sign test p=0.039, but magnitude CI [-3, +37] pp crosses zero. Reported as suggestive.\n\n**Additional observations (demoted):** FM2 (cross-dimension interference) and FM3 (overcorrection rebound) are acknowledged as unreliable/confounded and not presented as confirmed mechanisms.\n\n**Methods:**\n- τ-bench retail domain, 20 multi-turn tool-calling tasks, 16 tools\n- 3 behavioral dimensions: completion rate, politeness score, question rate\n- 6 BCP variants per dialect: baseline, AAVE, Indian English, overcorrected, single-dimension (2)\n- DPO training on Qwen2.5-7B with dialect-calibrated preference pairs (LoRA, DPO-003)\n- 4 independent runs × 20 tasks = 80 episodes per condition\n- BCa bootstrap with 10,000 resamples, stratified by run\n- Thresholds: 80% baseline retention (completion), 30% gap reduction (calibration)\n\n**Key tables:**\n- Table 1: 7B Base BCP completion/politeness across 4 runs\n- Table 2: 7B vs 14B comparison\n- Table 3: DPO-BCP interaction\n- Table 4: Bootstrap 95% CI summary (7 comparisons)\n- Table 5: Mechanism survival scorecard\n\n**Limitations acknowledged:** Single model family (Qwen2.5), single task domain (τ-bench retail), 20 tasks per run (wide CIs), missing 14B DPO experiment, underpowered secondary findings.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference\n\nThe paper was designed with the following experiments:\n- **plan_005b (7B BCP Multi-Run Replication):** 3 additional runs of 7B base BCP. Result: FM1 confirmed (aave retention 35.1%, indian 44.5%), FM2 exposed as high-variance artifact (SD=13.1%), FM3 collapsed (mean +0.4pp, 2/4 direction). Multi-run data undermined FM2/FM3 as named mechanisms.\n- **plan_005 (Bootstrap CI):** BCa bootstrap on existing data. FM1 CI excludes zero. FM3 CI [-2, 16.5]pp crosses zero. FM4 CI [-3, 37]pp crosses zero. Scale completion CI [1, 49]pp excludes zero but very wide. H2 gap reduction CI [12, 28]% — even upper bound below 30% threshold.\n- **plan_006 (14B DPO+BCP):** NOT EXECUTED due to GPU unavailability. FM4 reversal only documented at 7B scale. This is a significant gap — the most novel claim has the weakest evidence base.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed R1 review focused on:\n\n1. **Experimental design:** Are the experiments well-designed? Are the controls adequate? Is the evaluation methodology sound?\n2. **Statistical analysis:** Are the bootstrap CI methods appropriate? Are the thresholds (80% retention, 30% gap reduction) well-justified? Is the sample size (n=20 per run, 80 per condition) sufficient?\n3. **Results interpretation:** Do the authors overclaim or underclaim? Is the distinction between \"confirmed\" and \"suggestive\" findings appropriate?\n4. **Reproducibility:** Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are the DPO training details sufficient?\n5. **Missing experiments:** Impact of the missing 14B DPO experiment. Any other missing ablations?\n6. **Weaknesses in experimental coverage:** Single model family, single domain, etc.\n\nFormat your output as:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, numbered W1, W2, etc.]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Bulleted list]\n\n### Detailed Comments\n[Section-by-section analysis]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n\n### Confidence\n[1-5 scale: 5=absolutely certain, 4=quite sure, 3=fairly confident, 2=willing to be convinced, 1=guessing]\n```\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01QVHYMQURvYjtfTdeopR3PP","session_id":"ed833f14-0b34-43bd-911c-a079ebf54ca0","uuid":"0ffa4449-c7cd-4006-b030-1550c1f213c6","timestamp":"2026-05-13T16:28:59.541Z"}
