{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Y2M61qQVixTzQL51jxqprv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XhNXCVuX9w4d7BoK3D2U6z","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_2/projects/demo_calibrated_sim/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T153032/median/review_report.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5311,"cache_read_input_tokens":63862,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5311,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01QVHYMQURvYjtfTdeopR3PP","session_id":"ed833f14-0b34-43bd-911c-a079ebf54ca0","uuid":"f3d7046a-4285-4d2c-acfd-8c35b23ed6e3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XhNXCVuX9w4d7BoK3D2U6z","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260513T153032\n3\ttier: median\n4\toverall_score: borderline reject\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-13T16:26:49.594527+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: \"Why Calibration Fails: A Taxonomy of Failure Modes in Dialect-Aware LLM User Simulation\"\n9\t\n10\t**Dream ID:** 20260513T153032\n11\t**Target Venue:** EMNLP 2026 Findings (via ACL Rolling Review)\n12\t**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers, 4-step roundtable)\n13\t**Scoring Basis:** ARR review form — Soundness (1–5), Excitement (1–5), Overall Assessment (1–5 with half-points)\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## Consolidated Scores\n18\t\n19\t| Dimension | R1 (Methods+Experiments) | R2 (Novelty+Related Work) | Consensus |\n20\t|-----------|-------------------------|--------------------------|-----------|\n21\t| Soundness | 3.0 | 3.0 | **3.0** |\n22\t| Excitement | 3.0 | 2.5 | **2.5** |\n23\t| Overall | 2.5 | 2.5 | **2.5** |\n24\t| Confidence | 4.0 | 4.0 | **4.0** |\n25\t\n26\t**Headline:** Borderline Reject — genuine novelty in FM3/FM4 and scale analysis, but overclaimed framing, thin related work, and statistical limitations weaken the current submission.\n27\t\n28\t---\n29\t\n30\t## 1. Paper Summary\n31\t\n32\tThe paper investigates why behavioral calibration of LLM user simulators fails. Two strategies are studied: Behavioral Calibration Prompting (BCP, inference-time) and DPO (training-time), on Qwen2.5-7B/14B in the τ-bench retail domain. The paper identifies three failure mechanisms—FM1 (global behavior suppression: completion drops to 52.7% of baseline), FM2 (cross-dimension interference: −23.7% nontarget shift), FM3 (overcorrection rebound: +7.2pp opposite-direction drift)—and one interaction effect (DPO reverses BCP direction, 10/12 variant×run). The central finding is a \"safety-efficacy gap\": scaling 7B→14B eliminates destructive side effects (FM1, FM3) but does not improve calibration effectiveness.\n33\t\n34\t---\n35\t\n36\t## 2. Strengths (Consensus)\n37\t\n38\t**S1. FM4 (DPO-BCP interaction reversal) is a genuinely novel empirical finding.**\n39\tNo prior work documents that DPO training reverses the direction of prompt-based behavioral calibration. The 10/12 directional consistency (sign test p=0.006) at 7B is striking: BCP alone pushes AAVE politeness gap in the correct direction (−9.1pp) while DPO+BCP reverses it (+6.4pp). This goes beyond simply confirming Lee et al. (2024)'s \"DPO bypasses capabilities\" — it demonstrates a specific adversarial interaction in a calibration context. (R1, R2 agree)\n40\t\n41\t**S2. FM3 (overcorrection rebound) is novel and non-obvious.**\n42\tThe finding that extreme calibration targets cause opposite-direction drift is not predicted by prior work. The 4/4 run consistency at 7B (per-run rebound: +9.5, +4.2, +4.9, +10.4pp for DPO model) and clean disappearance at 14B (0/4 runs) adds an informative scale-dependent dimension. (R1, R2 agree)\n43\t\n44\t**S3. Well-structured hypothesis-gated evaluation protocol.**\n45\tThe H1/H2/H3 framework with pre-specified thresholds (H2: ≥30% gap reduction, H3: ≥80% completion retention) is commendable for a negative-results paper. It prevents post-hoc rationalization and makes the \"failure\" verdict falsifiable. (R1)\n46\t\n47\t**S4. Scale-dependent analysis reveals a genuinely interesting pattern.**\n48\tThe 7B \"catastrophic over-response\" vs. 14B \"selective ignoring\" dichotomy is more informative than a simple replication study. Both scales fail H2 but for qualitatively different reasons. The FM1 scale dependence is stark: AAVE completion retention 28.5% at 7B vs. 97.4% at 14B (Δ=68.9pp, CI: [42, 88]pp). (R1, R2 agree)\n49\t\n50\t**S5. Honest framing of contribution boundaries.**\n51\tFM1 is explicitly linked to persona-accuracy tradeoff (Basil et al. 2025, Hu et al. 2026); FM2 to bias spillover (Gupta et al. 2026). The DPO-BCP interaction is scoped to 7B with an explicit limitation paragraph. This intellectual honesty is noted positively by both reviewers. (R2, R1 agree)\n52\t\n53\t**S6. Multi-run replication with full per-run data transparency.**\n54\tThe paper reports 4 independent runs with per-run results (Table 6), enabling full reviewer verification. Directional consistency is reported systematically (Table 2). (R1)\n55\t\n56\t---\n57\t\n58\t## 3. Weaknesses (Consensus)\n59\t\n60\t**W1. The \"taxonomy\" framing significantly overclaims novelty. [Major]**\n61\tTwo of four entries (FM1, FM2) are self-acknowledged instances of known phenomena (persona-accuracy tradeoff, bias spillover) applied to a new context. The evidence comes from one model family (Qwen2.5) on one benchmark (τ-bench retail). \"Taxonomy\" implies comprehensive, generalizable classification; this is more accurately an \"empirical characterization\" or \"case study.\" The word \"taxonomy\" appears in the title, abstract, contributions, and conclusion and should be downgraded or explicitly qualified (e.g., \"toward a taxonomy\" or scoped to Qwen2.5). (R1, R2 agree)\n62\t\n63\t**W2. Related work is seriously underweight for EMNLP. [Major]**\n64\tThe submitted draft has only 9 references—far below the 30–50 typical for comparable EMNLP empirical analysis papers. Critical omissions include:\n65\t- **Controllable text generation** (FUDGE, PPLM, DExperts, GeDi): FM2 is fundamentally a multi-attribute controllable generation problem. These methods offer per-dimension control that could address FM2 by design. Not discussing them makes the paper appear unaware of an entire subfield.\n66\t- **Multi-objective alignment** (Rewarded Soups, Controlled Decoding from LMs): FM2's cross-dimension interference is a known challenge with proposed solutions.\n67\t- **Dialect/sociolinguistic NLP** (DialectBench, ReDial, EnDive): Zero dialect NLP benchmarks or surveys cited in a paper with \"Dialect-Aware\" in the title.\n68\t- **User simulation beyond τ-bench** (DAUS, User LMs, multi-turn RL for persona consistency).\n69\t- **DPO failure modes** (KTO, CRINGE, IPO): Would strengthen FM4 discussion.\n70\t- **Scaling laws for alignment** (Bai et al. 2022, McKenzie et al. 2023): The safety-efficacy gap claim needs contextualization. (R2, R1 partially agrees)\n71\t\n72\t**W3. The safety-efficacy gap may be a restatement of known scaling behavior rather than a novel finding. [Moderate]**\n73\tLarger models are known to be more robust to prompt perturbations (both beneficial and harmful). The observation that 14B \"selectively ignores\" BCP while 7B \"catastrophically over-responds\" may stem from the same underlying prompt robustness property, not from two separable dimensions (safety vs. efficacy). The paper frames this as a \"gap\" implying the two properties are separable, but the evidence is equally consistent with a single underlying mechanism (prompt sensitivity decreases with scale). (R2)\n74\t\n75\t**W4. Baseline completion of 0.351 at 7B confounds FM1 interpretation. [Moderate]**\n76\tOver 63% of tasks fail even without BCP. Adding *any* additional instruction to a capability-limited model would likely degrade performance further. FM1 may not be a BCP-specific failure mode but a general consequence of instruction-following overhead on a model that barely handles τ-bench. The 14B evidence supports this interpretation: when the model has sufficient capacity (0.617 baseline), BCP does not cause FM1. This reduces FM1 from a \"novel mechanism\" to \"known persona-accuracy tradeoff instantiated in a setting where the model was already struggling.\" (R1)\n77\t\n78\t**W5. H2 threshold of 30% is consequential and unjustified. [Moderate]**\n79\tThe bootstrap CI for the best observed gap reduction is [15.8%, 28.2%]. At a 25% threshold, H2 might pass. The entire \"calibration fails\" narrative hinges on this threshold choice. A sensitivity analysis showing at what threshold H2 begins to pass would strengthen the paper and enable readers to form their own conclusions. (R1)\n80\t\n81\t**W6. Missing 14B DPO+BCP experiment weakens the DPO-BCP interaction claim. [Moderate]**\n82\tFM4 is documented only at 7B scale. Without 14B DPO+BCP data, the paper cannot determine whether FM4 is also scale-dependent. The safety-efficacy gap characterization is incomplete: it covers BCP at both scales but DPO+BCP only at 7B. The paper honestly acknowledges this, but it remains a substantive gap. (R1, R2 agree)\n83\t\n84\t**W7. Wide confidence intervals throughout. [Minor-to-Moderate]**\n85\tThe DPO-BCP reversal CI is [+2, +32]pp—the magnitude is essentially unknown. FM3 DPO model CI lower bound is +0.5pp (barely excluding zero). The paper's strategy of emphasizing directional consistency over effect sizes is pragmatic but means the taxonomy provides qualitative rather than quantitative characterization. With n=4 runs per condition and n=20 tasks per run, statistical power is inherently limited. (R1)\n86\t\n87\t**W8. DPO training details are insufficient. [Minor]**\n88\tHow were preferred/dispreferred responses selected from the 1,563 preference pairs? What determined \"target behavioral rates\" in the traces? Were they paired within-dialogue or across-dialogue? Was there human validation of the preference labels? The LoRA configuration (q_proj and v_proj only, r=16) is stated but not justified. (R1)\n89\t\n90\t---\n91\t\n92\t## 4. Questions for Authors\n93\t\n94\t1. **H2 threshold sensitivity:** What is the minimum threshold at which H2 would pass for at least some conditions? A sensitivity curve (threshold vs. pass rate) would substantially strengthen the \"BCP fails\" claim.\n95\t\n96\t2. **Completion-conditioned analysis:** Among only completed episodes, does FM2 cross-dimension interference persist? This would separate \"BCP disrupts the model\" (FM1) from \"BCP causes dimensional coupling\" (the novel FM2 claim).\n97\t\n98\t3. **Overcorrected variant specification:** What are the exact target behavioral rates for the Overcorrected variant? Is FM3 rebound an artifact of unrealistically extreme targets, or does it occur with moderate directional targets?\n99\t\n100\t4. **14B prompt sensitivity test:** Does the 14B model respond to *any* behavioral prompt modifications (beyond BCP calibration)? If 14B resists all prompt-based behavioral steering, the safety-efficacy \"gap\" collapses to simple prompt robustness at scale.\n101\t\n102\t5. **FM4 incremental novelty:** What is the specific incremental contribution of FM4 beyond confirming Lee et al. (2024)'s prediction that DPO alignment is fragile and reversible under competing signals?\n103\t\n104\t6. **BCP variant equivalence:** Do the AAVE and Indian English BCP variants use different target behavioral rates, or identical rates with different dialect labels? If identical, the two conditions should be pooled or discussed as replicates.\n105\t\n106\t7. **Controllable generation alternatives:** Were FUDGE-style or reward-model-guided decoding approaches considered? If infeasible in the τ-bench tool-calling setting, stating this explicitly would strengthen the contribution.\n107\t\n108\t---\n109\t\n110\t## 5. Experiment Design Coverage\n111\t\n112\tCompared to the full experiment design:\n113\t\n114\t| Experiment | Status in Paper | Assessment |\n115\t|-----------|----------------|------------|\n116\t| 7B BCP Multi-Run (plan_005b) | ✅ Fully covered (Tables 1, 2, 6) | 4 runs reported with per-run data |\n117\t| Bootstrap CIs (plan_005) | ✅ Fully covered (Table 5) | All core CIs reported |\n118\t| 14B DPO+BCP (plan_006) | ❌ Not completed | Acknowledged as limitation; resource constraint credible |\n119\t\n120\tThe paper covers the two completed experiments thoroughly. The missing plan_006 is the primary gap, honestly acknowledged. The paper's scope limitation paragraph on DPO-BCP interaction (Section 4.2) is well-written and appropriately defensive.\n121\t\n122\t---\n123\t\n124\t## 6. Verdict and Recommendations\n125\t\n126\t### For EMNLP 2026 Findings\n127\t\n128\t**Current assessment: Borderline Reject (2.5/5)**\n129\t\n130\tThe paper has genuine contributions — FM3 and FM4 are novel empirical observations, and the scale-dependent analysis is informative. However, the submission in its current form has two major issues that place it below the Findings threshold:\n131\t\n132\t1. **Overclaimed framing** (\"taxonomy\" with 2/4 entries being known phenomena, from 1 model family on 1 benchmark)\n133\t2. **Thin related work** (9 references, missing entire subfields relevant to FM2)\n134\t\n135\t### Revision Path to Acceptance\n136\t\n137\tA revised version addressing the following could likely reach 3.0–3.5 (borderline accept to lean accept for Findings):\n138\t\n139\t1. **Reframe from \"taxonomy\" to \"empirical characterization\"** or scope explicitly to Qwen2.5 + τ-bench. Foreground FM3 and FM4 as novel contributions; position FM1 and FM2 explicitly as known phenomena studied in a new context.\n140\t\n141\t2. **Expand related work to 25–30 references.** Engage substantively with controllable text generation (FUDGE, DExperts, PPLM), multi-objective alignment (Rewarded Soups), dialect NLP (DialectBench, ReDial), and DPO failure modes (KTO, IPO).\n142\t\n143\t3. **Add H2 threshold sensitivity analysis.** Show at what threshold H2 begins to pass. This transforms an arbitrary binary verdict into a continuous characterization.\n144\t\n145\t4. **Discuss the prompt-robustness interpretation** of the safety-efficacy gap alongside the current \"gap\" framing. Acknowledge that both properties (reduced harm, reduced calibration) may stem from the same underlying mechanism.\n146\t\n147\t5. **Add completion-conditioned FM2 analysis** to separate the FM1 and FM2 contributions.\n148\t\n149\t6. **Include at least one figure** (forest plot of bootstrap CIs or scale-dependent severity heatmap).\n150\t\n151\t### Estimated Acceptance Probability\n152\t- Current form: ~25–35% for Findings\n153\t- After recommended revisions: ~50–60% for Findings\n154\t\n155\t---\n156\t\n157\t## Appendix A: R1 Individual Review (Methods + Experiments)\n158\t\n159\t### Key Points\n160\t- Soundness 3/5, Excitement 3/5, Overall 2.5/5\n161\t- H1/H2/H3 framework is well-designed and commendable for a negative-results paper\n162\t- FM3 (4/4 run consistency) and FM4 (10/12, sign test p=0.006) are the strongest empirical findings\n163\t- Baseline completion of 0.351 at 7B confounds FM1 — the model is already struggling with τ-bench independent of BCP\n164\t- H2 threshold of 30% is arbitrary and consequential; needs sensitivity analysis\n165\t- Wide CIs (e.g., DPO-BCP reversal [+2, +32]pp) reflect limited statistical power\n166\t- DPO preference pair construction insufficiently described\n167\t- Missing 14B DPO+BCP experiment (plan_006) leaves the interaction effect at single-scale\n168\t\n169\t### Assessment\n170\tThe experimental methodology is ambitious but uneven. FM3 and FM4 have adequate multi-run backing; the paper's emphasis on directional consistency over exact magnitudes is pragmatic given n=4 runs. The core methodological concern is the confound between model capability and calibration effect at 7B — FM1 may simply be instruction-following overhead on a model that already struggles with τ-bench, not a BCP-specific mechanism. The paper would benefit from foregrounding FM3/FM4 as the genuinely novel experimental contributions while positioning FM1/FM2 as known phenomena confirmed in a new setting.\n171\t\n172\t## Appendix B: R2 Individual Review (Novelty + Related Work)\n173\t\n174\t### Key Points\n175\t- Soundness 3/5, Excitement 2.5/5, Overall 2.5/5\n176\t- FM4 is genuinely novel; FM3 is novel and non-obvious; FM1/FM2 are known phenomena in new context\n177\t- \"Taxonomy\" framing overclaims — should be \"empirical characterization\" or scoped explicitly\n178\t- Related work is the paper's most significant weakness: 9 references, missing controllable generation (FUDGE, PPLM, DExperts), multi-objective alignment, dialect NLP benchmarks, DPO alternatives\n179\t- Safety-efficacy gap may be a restatement of known prompt-robustness scaling behavior\n180\t- No engagement with why existing controllable generation methods were not tried or discussed\n181\t- FM4's novelty is partially predictable from Lee et al. (2024)\n182\t\n183\t### Assessment\n184\tThe paper's novelty is distributed unevenly: FM3 and FM4 are genuine empirical discoveries, while FM1 and FM2 are contextual confirmations of known phenomena. The \"taxonomy\" framing sets expectations the evidence cannot meet. The related work section is the most actionable weakness — expanding from 9 to 25–30 references and engaging with controllable generation and multi-objective alignment would transform the paper's positioning. The safety-efficacy gap concept is interesting but needs more careful framing as a hypothesis rather than an established finding, given the single model family and plausible alternative explanation (general prompt robustness at scale).\n185\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01QVHYMQURvYjtfTdeopR3PP","session_id":"ed833f14-0b34-43bd-911c-a079ebf54ca0","uuid":"0e748a8c-b29c-40d2-8fea-74543e2bd0ab","timestamp":"2026-05-13T16:30:49.438Z"}
